# Lead

This is the first real unit of the Sales workbook, and it is where the whole app
gets its shape. Before we can ingest a lead, enrich it, score it, or rank it, we
need to say **what a lead is**. That definition is a *type*, and in this format a
type is not a separate declaration language — it is just **structure**, written
in ordinary Elixir.

## A unit is a named, addressable thing

Everything in a workbook is a **unit**: a named piece you can point at from
anywhere. This file defines a unit called [[Lead]]. Other files will refer to it
by name — they will write `%Lead{}` to mean "a value of this shape", and the
symbol resolver turns that mention into a real edge in the code graph. So when
:score writes `score(%Lead{})`, or :enrich returns a `%Lead{}`, those are not
loose conventions; they are typed references back to *this* unit.

A unit has no syntax of its own. It is a `do ... end` **block** in front of a
**kind word**. The kind word tells the weave what the block is. Here the kind is
`defmodule` — a plain Elixir module — and the module *is* the contract: its
public functions are exports, its structs and atoms are its types. There is no
`.wit` to write and none to read; the WebAssembly contract is generated from this
Elixir.

Notice there is **no placement** on this block. A struct definition has nothing to
run — it is a static definition, so it lives neither on the `client` nor the
`server`. Placement is for code that executes; a shape is just a shape.

## The block delimits itself — no fences

The code below is set off from this prose by a blank line above and a blank line
below, and it closes with its own `end`. That `end` is the boundary. There is no
triple-backtick, no HTML, no wrapper — the `do ... end` is self-delimiting, and the
kind word `defmodule` in front names what it is. Read top to bottom and the prose
and the code interleave cleanly.

defmodule Lead do
  @moduledoc """
  A sales lead as it moves through the pipeline.

  Every field carries a default, and the default's value is what the
  inference engine reads to assign the field's type — `name` is a string
  because `""` is a string, `revenue` is an integer because `0` is an
  integer. We never write a typespec for this; the structure is the type.
  """

  defstruct name: "",
            domain: "",
            revenue: 0,
            employees: 0,
            score: 0,
            status: :new

  @doc """
  Build a fresh lead from a name and domain. A lead always enters the
  pipeline as `:new`; :enrich and :score advance it from there.
  """
  def new(name, domain) when is_binary(name) and is_binary(domain) do
    %Lead{name: name, domain: domain, status: :new}
  end

  @doc "True once :enrich has filled the firmographic fields."
  def enriched?(%Lead{status: status}) do
    status in [:enriched, :scored, :contacted]
  end
end

## Types are structure, not a DSL

Look at what just happened. `defstruct` with defaults gave us a **record**: a
fixed set of named fields, each typed by the value standing in front of it. That
is the entire type declaration. We did **not** write `type Lead = record(...)`,
and we did **not** lead with `@type` or `@spec` — Elixir's old typespecs are being
retired in favour of an inference-only, set-theoretic system, so the *defaults
are the source of truth*. An annotation, if we ever add one, is an optional
precision layer on top, never the thing that defines the shape.

## The enum is a set of atoms

A lead has a lifecycle. We represent the allowed states as a plain set of
**atoms** — that is what an enum is here. No enum keyword, no class; just the
atoms a `status` field is allowed to hold.

@statuses [:new, :enriched, :scored, :contacted]

These four atoms are the spine of the whole app. A lead is born `:new`; :enrich
advances it to `:enriched`; :score advances it to `:scored`; the analyst agent's
outreach moves it to `:contacted`. Because they are atoms, they are also
*queryable references* — a board can filter on `:scored`, and the dataflow edges
that carry a `%Lead{}` from one unit to the next know which status each step is
meant to leave behind. The small helper below is the one place we keep that
ordering honest, so no downstream unit has to re-encode it.

defmodule Lead.Status do
  @statuses [:new, :enriched, :scored, :contacted]

  @doc "The canonical lifecycle order, used for validation and sorting."
  def all, do: @statuses

  @doc "The status that should follow the current one, or itself when terminal."
  def next(current) when current in @statuses do
    case Enum.drop_while(@statuses, &(&1 != current)) do
      [^current, following | _] -> following
      _ -> current
    end
  end

  @doc "Guard the document against a status nothing in the pipeline can produce."
  def valid?(status), do: status in @statuses
end

## How the rest of the app reaches this unit

From here on, every other file leans on this definition without redefining it:

- **:score** takes a `%Lead{}` and returns an integer — it reads `revenue` and
  `employees` straight off this struct and writes back `score`.
- **:enrich** returns a `%Lead{}` with `domain`, `revenue`, and `employees`
  filled and `status: :enriched`.
- **:pipeline** threads a list of `%Lead{}` through enrich, score, and rank,
  watching the `@statuses` enum advance.
- The board and dashboard render values of this struct directly, grouping by the
  same `status` atoms.

Those are all **typed edges** in the code graph — `:score`, `%Lead{}`, and
`:enriched` in the paragraphs above are not decoration, they resolve against the
workspace symbol table back to the names defined in this file. Tag this unit
#foundation so `work near #foundation` surfaces it as the root the others build
on. Next the app puts this shape to work in [[Score]].
